export const systems:Lesson[]=[
{id:7,start:{"problem": "One processor must service a button and display while a calculation keeps running.", "outcome": "Explain how tasks take turns without losing their saved values, and why some tasks must wait.", "firstExperiment": ["Open Context switch and press Next event once to inspect A’s saved hardware frame.", "Continue until B’s context is restored; compare A’s R4 = 41 with B’s R4 = 92.", "Open Priority inversion and turn Priority inheritance on; watch High’s waiting work drop from 11 units to 3."]},shortTitle:'Sharing the CPU between tasks',subtitle:'Real-time operating system: sharing one processor between tasks',prerequisites:[0,3,5,6],youKnow:['An interrupt pauses ordinary code and preserves a return path.','SysTick is a hardware timer that supplies regular interrupts. SRAM is writable working memory; a stack stores local values and return addresses.'],adds:['Separate task contexts and stacks','A scheduler, PendSV, sleep, mutexes and queues','Priority inversion and inheritance'],why:'A calculation that never returns must not prevent a button or display deadline from being serviced.',topics:[
topic("Pause one task and resume another","The CPU can do one instruction at a time. A task is one piece of work. To switch tasks safely, the system saves where the old task stopped and the values it was using—like putting a bookmark in a book—then restores the next task’s bookmark.",'Without a task switch, one job that never finishes can block every job behind it. An operating system can pause one task and give the CPU to another ready task.',"Task A is paused. The CPU automatically saves some of A’s values on A’s stack. The operating system then asks PendSV (a special “switch later” event) to run. PendSV saves A’s remaining values, loads B’s saved values and stack, then returns. Now B continues from its own bookmark.",'Hardware frame: r0–r3, r12, LR, PC, xPSR. PendSV saves r4–r11. Task code uses PSP; exception handlers execute using MSP. EXC_RETURN 0xFFFFFFFD returns to Thread mode on PSP. These are preserved register values, not copies of entire program memory.','Step the switch. Before restoring B, predict which task’s R4 should appear.',source(7,'os.c'),'SCB->ICSR = SCB_ICSR_PENDSVSET_Msk;',["Task A runs with its own saved work values and stack.", "An interrupt pauses A. The processor automatically saves eight key values on A’s stack.", "The operating system requests a low-priority switch called PendSV. It waits until urgent interrupt work is finished.", "PendSV saves A’s remaining values and stack position, then loads B’s saved values and stack position.", "The switch ends by restoring B’s remaining values, so B continues at the instruction where it paused."],"Save the old task before loading the next one. Each task has its own saved place."),
topic("Choose a task that can run","The scheduler is the OS function that chooses the next task. A ready task can run now; a sleeping task waits for a time; a blocked task waits for data or a resource. Priority means urgency, but only a ready task is eligible.",'Busy-waiting consumes CPU that another task could use. An urgent ready task must be able to run.',"A timer tick advances the OS time. Sleeping tasks whose wake time has arrived become ready. The scheduler ignores tasks still sleeping or blocked. It selects the ready task with the greatest task-priority number; equals take turns. A switch is requested if the selected task differs from the running task.",'This kernel uses larger task priority numbers for higher urgency. NVIC uses smaller exception priority numbers for higher urgency: these are different namespaces. os_delay_until advances an absolute time grid; os_delay schedules relative to now.','Change task priority or put a task to sleep. Explain why the winner changes.',source(7,'os.c'),'if (t->state == OS_READY && (best == 0 || t->prio > best->prio)) {\n    best = t;\n}',["A timer tick advances the OS time.", "Sleeping tasks whose wake time has arrived become ready.", "The scheduler ignores tasks still sleeping or blocked.", "It selects the ready task with the greatest task-priority number; equals take turns.", "A switch is requested if the selected task differs from the running task."],"Choose from ready tasks first, then compare their priorities."),
topic("Let one task finish using shared hardware","A mutex is an ownership record that allows one task at a time to use a shared resource, such as serial output. A task that cannot obtain it blocks. Priority inversion happens when urgent work waits for a less urgent owner, while unrelated work delays that owner.",'Two printf writers can mix their characters into an unusable line. A long interrupt mask would delay every interrupt.',"Low owns the mutex while writing a complete output line. High requests that mutex and blocks because Low has not released it. Without inheritance, Medium outranks Low and runs first, extending High’s wait. With priority inheritance, Low temporarily receives High’s priority and finishes before Medium. Low releases the mutex, returns to its original priority, and High can become ready.",'os_mutex_lock sets OS_BLOCKED and waiting_on. os_mutex_unlock restores base_prio and wakes waiters. This course kernel handles its demonstrated single-mutex pattern; it does not implement general nested priority inheritance.','Run the identical High/Medium/Low scenario with inheritance off and on. Find the events that add avoidable waiting.',source(7,'os.c'),'if (m->owner->prio < os_current->prio) { m->owner->prio = os_current->prio; }',["Low owns the mutex while writing a complete output line.", "High requests that mutex and blocks because Low has not released it.", "Without inheritance, Medium outranks Low and runs first, extending High’s wait.", "With priority inheritance, Low temporarily receives High’s priority and finishes before Medium.", "Low releases the mutex, returns to its original priority, and High can become ready."],"The waiting task needs the owner to finish; inheritance lets that owner run sooner."),
topic("Copy messages into a queue","A queue is storage with a fixed number of message slots. A producer puts a copy in; a consumer removes the oldest copy. This lets tasks exchange data without both modifying the same buffer at once. An interrupt service routine (ISR) must finish promptly and cannot sleep waiting for space.",'If both tasks modify one buffer, the writer can replace data before the reader finishes. Copying into queue slots preserves unread messages, and blocking lets the processor run other work.',"A task trying to read an empty queue blocks until data is available. A producer copies a message into a free slot. The queue wakes a waiting reader, which can then be scheduled. The reader removes the oldest message and frees one slot. If an ISR writes when every slot is occupied, it returns failure rather than waiting.",'os_queue_t stores fixed-size items with head and count. Critical sections save PRIMASK, disable interrupts, then restore prior PRIMASK so nested callers remain correct.','Push more items than queue capacity; observe the ISR drop. Consume and see blocked reader wake.',source(7,'os.c'),'if (q->count == q->capacity) { leave(pm); return 0; }',["A task trying to read an empty queue blocks until data is available.", "A producer copies a message into a free slot.", "The queue wakes a waiting reader, which can then be scheduled.", "The reader removes the oldest message and frees one slot.", "If an ISR writes when every slot is occupied, it returns failure rather than waiting."],"A queue stores copies; a full queue makes an ISR drop or report failure, never wait."),
topic("Reserve enough stack memory","Each task’s stack is a reserved region of static random-access memory (SRAM), the chip’s writable working memory. Function calls and saved registers use that space. The OS also builds a starting register frame for a new task, so the ordinary restore procedure can begin its first instruction.",'Twelve kilobytes of SRAM must serve all tasks. A stack can overwrite valid neighboring RAM without trapping.',"Task creation reserves a stack and fills its unused words with 0xDEADBEEF, a recognizable marker. It builds a register frame containing the task’s entry address and initial processor state. The first restore loads that frame and starts the task function. Calls, local values, and interruptions consume more stack space. Counting unchanged fill words estimates the deepest past use; exhausting the allocation can overwrite neighboring memory.",'Initial xPSR.T must be one (0x01000000). PC points to task entry; LR points to task_exit. Eight-byte alignment is enforced. High-water marks measure past use, not proof that future paths fit.','Choose 96 stack words against an illustrative 115-word demand. Predict corruption rather than guaranteed HardFault.',source(7,'os.c'),'for (uint32_t i = 0; i < stack_words; i++) { stack[i] = OS_STACK_FILL; }',["Task creation reserves a stack and fills its unused words with 0xDEADBEEF, a recognizable marker.", "It builds a register frame containing the task’s entry address and initial processor state.", "The first restore loads that frame and starts the task function.", "Calls, local values, and interruptions consume more stack space.", "Counting unchanged fill words estimates the deepest past use; exhausting the allocation can overwrite neighboring memory."],"A stack limit is a software allocation; crossing it into valid SRAM may not trigger a fault.")],prediction:{question:'High waits for a mutex held by Low. Medium needs no mutex. Why can Medium delay High?',options:['Medium owns the mutex too','Medium preempts Low, preventing Low from unlocking','High loses its configured priority'],correct:1,explanations:['Only Low owns it. Medium delays its owner indirectly.','Exactly: High cannot run until Low releases. Inheritance lets Low finish ahead of Medium.','High remains high priority but is blocked, so it cannot be selected.']},recap:['Saving context preserves where a task was, not only which function it is.','Choosing a task and switching to it are separate actions.','Priority inheritance removes unrelated Medium interference; it does not erase Low’s necessary critical work.'],terms:[{term:'RTOS',meaning:'Real-time operating system: manages tasks and synchronization so timing can be designed.'},{term:'PendSV',meaning:'Software-pended exception used here for deferred context switching.'},{term:'PSP',meaning:'Process stack pointer: register containing the current task’s stack position. MSP, the main stack pointer, is used by exception handlers.'},{term:'Priority inversion',meaning:'High-priority work waits indirectly for unrelated lower-priority work.'},{term:"OS / RTOS",meaning:"An operating system manages tasks; a real-time OS supplies scheduling and coordination for work with timing requirements."},{term:"Scheduler",meaning:"Code that chooses which ready task runs next; the context-switch code then installs that task’s saved values."},{term:"Thread mode",meaning:"Processor mode used by ordinary task code, as opposed to handler mode used by exception code."},{term:"Context",meaning:"The register values and stack position needed to resume paused execution."},{term:"Mutex",meaning:"An ownership record allowing one task at a time to use a shared resource."},{term:"Critical section",meaning:"A short operation protected from interference; this kernel temporarily masks interrupts and restores their prior mask."},{term:"NVIC / PRIMASK",meaning:"Nested vectored interrupt controller chooses exceptions; PRIMASK records whether configurable interrupts are masked."}],sources:[reference,arm,cmsis],limitations:['Context switches are event-level educational models, not measured instruction timing.','Course measured kernel runs used Renode STM32F072 with test startup and UART adaptation; not real C031 silicon measurements.','Single-mutex inheritance only; no claim of general RTOS completeness.']},
{id:8,start:{"problem": "The board and computer exchange bytes that can arrive during other work or become damaged.", "outcome": "Trace one byte into a checked message and distinguish a received byte from a safely stored byte.", "firstExperiment": ["In Wire waveform leave Character set to A; compare byte 0x41 with data bit d0.", "Use Next event to follow the start bit, eight data bits, and stop bit.", "Open Frame damage and choose Corrupted body byte; compare Sender frame, On receiver, and the rejection."]},shortTitle:'Sending messages to a computer',subtitle:'UART and Python: exchange and validate messages',prerequisites:[5,6,7],youKnow:['An interrupt service routine (ISR) runs when hardware requests attention and must collect data promptly.','Queues can wake a sleeping task; mutexes preserve a whole output line.'],adds:['A byte as a timed wire signal','Interrupt-driven RX and TX buffering','Printable checksummed messages and Python host processing'],why:'A PC and a microcontroller need a reliable conversation despite asynchronous bytes and limited buffers.',topics:[
topic("Send eight bits with agreed timing","A byte is eight bits, each either 0 or 1. UART means universal asynchronous receiver/transmitter: hardware that sends bytes as timed voltage levels without a separate clock wire. Both ends must agree on bit duration and byte format before a character such as A can be reconstructed.",'A byte in memory is not automatically meaningful to another device. The start bit tells the receiver when to sample.',"The wire rests at a high level between bytes. The sender drives it low for one start bit, which tells the receiver when to begin timing. Eight data bits follow from least significant to most significant: bit 0 is first. A high stop bit ends this byte. The receiver samples at the agreed times and rebuilds the eight-bit value.",'Course USART2 uses PA2 TX and PA3 RX, AF1, 115200 baud, 8N1. BRR=(48000000+115200/2)/115200=417. Ten bit times per byte ≈86.8 µs at requested baud; divider yields a slightly different actual baud.','Transmit A (0x41). Identify why its first data bit is 1, although written binary begins with 0.',source(8,'uart.c'),'USART2->BRR = (pclk + baud / 2u) / baud;',["The wire rests at a high level between bytes.", "The sender drives it low for one start bit, which tells the receiver when to begin timing.", "Eight data bits follow from least significant to most significant: bit 0 is first.", "A high stop bit ends this byte.", "The receiver samples at the agreed times and rebuilds the eight-bit value."],"UART sends bit 0 first; the start bit establishes timing for that byte."),
topic("Store incoming and outgoing bytes separately","Receive (RX) and transmit (TX) are different directions. Incoming bytes need storage even while a task is busy. Outgoing bytes wait in another buffer while UART hardware sends the earlier byte. A ring buffer reuses fixed slots by wrapping read and write positions back to the beginning.",'A long printf can take milliseconds. Incoming bytes cannot wait for that task to finish.',"Hardware receives a byte and raises RXNE, the receive-data-ready flag. The ISR reads the receive data register RDR and copies the byte into the course’s 64-byte RX queue. A task later removes RX bytes and builds a message. For output, one producer task at a time places bytes in the TX ring and enables its empty-transmit-register interrupt; the ISR alone advances the consumer position. Each TX interrupt writes one byte to hardware; when no bytes remain, it disables that interrupt.",'TX ring reserves one slot to distinguish empty and full, so usable capacity is 255. Tasks are serialized by print mutex at line level. TXEIE is enabled when work arrives and disabled when ring empties. Reading RDR clears RXNE; ORE is cleared via ICR.','Shrink RX capacity and inject a burst. Compare ISR receipt with queue loss; software drops need not set ORE.',source(8,'uart.c'),'if (!os_queue_put_from_isr(&rx_queue, &c)) {\n    stats.rx_dropped++;\n}',["Hardware receives a byte and raises RXNE, the receive-data-ready flag.", "The ISR reads the receive data register RDR and copies the byte into the course’s 64-byte RX queue.", "A task later removes RX bytes and builds a message.", "For output, one producer task at a time places bytes in the TX ring and enables its empty-transmit-register interrupt; the ISR alone advances the consumer position.", "Each TX interrupt writes one byte to hardware; when no bytes remain, it disables that interrupt."],"Hardware receipt does not guarantee software storage: a full RX queue can still lose a byte."),
topic("Check a complete message before acting","A frame is one complete message with markers that identify its beginning and end. A checksum is a value calculated from its contents and sent alongside them. The receiver calculates the value again; a mismatch shows that the received message differs and should be rejected.",'Missing or corrupted bytes can silently change a command. The receiver should refuse damaged commands.',"The sender creates a printable body such as LED,85. It combines the body’s ASCII byte values using XOR, a bit operation that returns 1 where bits differ. It sends a dollar marker, body, star marker, two checksum digits, and a line ending. The receiver finds the markers and recalculates the checksum from the received body. It rejects a malformed or mismatched frame before applying the command; some paired errors can cancel and pass this weak check.",'LED,85 gives $LED,85*6C; successful reply $ACK,LED,85*09. XOR detects every single-bit error but can miss paired flips that cancel. This is NMEA-style framing, not a CRC or authentication.','Corrupt a byte, remove a byte, or replace the checksum. Predict the specific rejection.',source(8,'proto.c'),'for (size_t i = 0; i < len; i++) { cs ^= (uint8_t)body[i]; }',["The sender creates a printable body such as LED,85.", "It combines the body’s ASCII byte values using XOR, a bit operation that returns 1 where bits differ.", "It sends a dollar marker, body, star marker, two checksum digits, and a line ending.", "The receiver finds the markers and recalculates the checksum from the received body.", "It rejects a malformed or mismatched frame before applying the command; some paired errors can cancel and pass this weak check."],"Check the received body before applying its command; XOR detects some damage, not every change."),
topic("Check received data on the computer","The host is the computer communicating with the board. Telemetry means measurements the board sends out. The Python host reads messages, checks their format and checksum, and then interprets fields such as a reading or sequence number. This separates useful data from damaged lines and startup text.",'Seeing text scroll is not the same as knowing data was received intact or on time.',"Read a line from the serial port or a saved capture file. Recognize whether it is a framed message or unrelated text. Validate the frame before decoding its data fields. Count accepted and rejected frames, and compare sequence numbers for missing entries. Display or chart numeric telemetry; investigate gaps without assuming their cause.",'host.py --frame "LED,170" emits $LED,170*57. --file capture.txt checks a recording; --selftest checks a known GPS sentence. LED, rate, quiet and stats are board commands; --chart selects numeric telemetry fields. A sequence gap suggests missing data, not proof of its cause.','Generate a frame in the lab and inspect the exact host decision. Follow the source link to run host.py on a captured log.',source(8,'host.py'),undefined,["Read a line from the serial port or a saved capture file.", "Recognize whether it is a framed message or unrelated text.", "Validate the frame before decoding its data fields.", "Count accepted and rejected frames, and compare sequence numbers for missing entries.", "Display or chart numeric telemetry; investigate gaps without assuming their cause."],"A line arriving is only the first step: validate it, then interpret its fields.")],prediction:{question:'The ISR receives all bytes but its queue is full. What happens?',options:['No loss because an interrupt ran','The ISR waits for the task','The byte is dropped and rx_dropped increases'],correct:2,explanations:['Receipt into hardware and storage in software are different steps.','An ISR must not block waiting for its consumer task.','Correct. Checksum framing may catch the resulting damaged message.']},recap:['UART is a timed byte stream; framing defines messages.','Hardware overrun and software queue overflow are different failures.','A checksum can reject damage without identifying its physical cause.'],terms:[{term:'Baud',meaning:'Signal symbols per second; in this UART one symbol is one bit.'},{term:'8N1',meaning:'Eight data bits, no parity, one stop bit.'},{term:'Ring buffer',meaning:'Bounded storage whose read/write positions wrap around.'},{term:'Checksum',meaning:'Small calculated check value used to detect some data damage.'},{term:"Byte / ASCII",meaning:"A byte is eight bits. ASCII assigns numeric byte codes to characters such as A = 65 = 0x41."},{term:"UART / USART",meaning:"Universal asynchronous receiver/transmitter; USART also supports synchronous modes, but this course uses its asynchronous UART mode."},{term:"RX / TX",meaning:"Receive, data arriving; transmit, data leaving."},{term:"Telemetry",meaning:"Measurements and status sent by the board for another system to inspect."},{term:"XOR",meaning:"Exclusive OR: each output bit is 1 when the corresponding input bits differ."},{term:"RXNE / TXE / ORE",meaning:"Receive register has unread data / transmit register is empty / hardware receive overrun before the prior byte was read."},{term:"RDR / ICR",meaning:"Receive data register holds the incoming byte; interrupt clear register accepts writes that clear flags such as overrun."}],sources:[reference,cmsis,'https://docs.python.org/3/library/argparse.html'],limitations:['Waveform timing is a slowed educational representation, not a captured oscilloscope trace.','Browser host display models protocol decisions; it does not open a real serial port or execute Python.','XOR checksum is intentionally weak; paired errors may pass.']},
{id:9,start:{"problem": "Software must read a knob voltage, ask a motion sensor for data, and send commands to a display.", "outcome": "Distinguish voltage conversion from digital bus transfers and identify what each acknowledgement proves.", "firstExperiment": ["In ADC voltage leave PA4 input at 1.65 V and note the 12-bit DR reading.", "Reduce Reference VDDA from 3.30 V to 3.00 V while leaving input unchanged; watch DR rise.", "Open I²C register read and uncheck Sensor connected; use Next event to find where address rejection stops the read."]},shortTitle:'Reading sensors and talking to devices',subtitle:'Sensors and buses: convert voltage and transfer digital data',prerequisites:[5,7,8],youKnow:['Pins can be assigned alternate functions.','A bus is a connection carrying data between components. A mutex permits one task at a time to use it.'],adds:['Voltage quantization with ADC','Addressed I²C transactions and ACK','SPI chip selection and clocked shifts'],why:'A controller must turn real-world voltage, motion and display commands into information it can use.',topics:[
topic("Convert voltage into a digital reading","An analog-to-digital converter (ADC) measures an input voltage and returns an integer code. Its reference voltage determines the full measuring range. A 12-bit ADC has 4096 possible codes, so nearby voltages within the same measurement bin produce the same number.",'Software calculates with numbers; a potentiometer supplies a continuous voltage.',"The input pin receives the knob’s voltage. After ADC initialization and calibration, a sample captures that voltage. Conversion compares the sample with the reference range and selects one of 4096 codes. Software reads the result from the data register DR. Keeping the input fixed while reducing the reference increases its fraction of the range, so the code rises until it saturates.",'Code range 0–4095; educational ideal bins use floor(Vin/VDDA ×4096), clamped. Approximate voltage reconstruction commonly uses code×VDDA/4095. Actual transfer errors, noise and reference tolerance remain. adc_init enables clock/regulator, waits, calibrates while disabled, then enables and waits for ADRDY.','Keep input at 1.65 V and change VDDA from 3.3 to 3.0 V. Predict code direction.',source(9,'adc.c'),'ADC1->CR |= ADC_CR_ADCAL;',["The input pin receives the knob’s voltage.", "After ADC initialization and calibration, a sample captures that voltage.", "Conversion compares the sample with the reference range and selects one of 4096 codes.", "Software reads the result from the data register DR.", "Keeping the input fixed while reducing the reference increases its fraction of the range, so the code rises until it saturates."],"The ADC code describes input divided by reference; changing either changes the reading."),
topic("Estimate the actual supply voltage","VDDA is the ADC’s analog supply and reference voltage here. VREFINT is an internal reference with a factory calibration reading stored in the chip. Measuring this known internal reference lets software estimate today’s supply instead of assuming it is exactly 3.3 volts.",'A sagging battery makes a fixed assumed 3.3 V conversion wrong.',"Read the factory calibration value measured at a known 3000 mV supply. Measure the internal reference with the ADC at today’s supply. Reject a blank calibration value or a zero reading to avoid an invalid calculation. Compare the old and new readings to calculate the present supply. A falling supply gives the same internal voltage a larger share of the ADC range, so its raw reading rises.",'Course uses VREFINT_CAL at 0x1FFF756A, calibrated at 3000 mV: VDDA≈3000×cal/raw. It checks 0, 0xFFFF and raw<=0. Internal reference and channel selection require readiness waits.','Explain why reference ADC code rises when supply falls.',source(9,'Main.c'),undefined,["Read the factory calibration value measured at a known 3000 mV supply.", "Measure the internal reference with the ADC at today’s supply.", "Reject a blank calibration value or a zero reading to avoid an invalid calculation.", "Compare the old and new readings to calculate the present supply.", "A falling supply gives the same internal voltage a larger share of the ADC range, so its raw reading rises."],"For the same internal voltage, a higher ADC reading indicates a lower supply."),
topic("Read a register from an addressed device","A bus is a shared connection that transfers data between chips. I²C, inter-integrated circuit, uses a clock wire and a data wire. A device address chooses which chip responds; a register address chooses a value inside that chip. ACK means acknowledge: the receiver accepted that stage of the transfer.",'Without addressing, several connected chips would not know which one should respond.',"The controller starts a transaction and sends the sensor address with a write direction. The sensor acknowledges, then accepts register address 0x75: its identity register. The controller issues a repeated start and the sensor address with a read direction. The sensor returns its identity byte. The controller sends NACK, not acknowledge, to request no further byte, then STOP; an earlier address NACK means no device accepted the request.",'PB8/PB9 AF6 connect I2C1 SCL/SDA. Open-drain outputs need pull-ups. C0 I2C CR2 sets SADD, NBYTES, RD_WRN and START. Driver bounds waits; NACK or timeout aborts. MPU6050 address is 0x68 or 0x69, WHO_AM_I register 0x75 returns 0x68. Clear PWR_MGMT_1.SLEEP before motion data.', 'Disconnect the device using ACK toggle. Step to NACK and explain why software must stop.',source(9,'i2c.c'),undefined,["The controller starts a transaction and sends the sensor address with a write direction.", "The sensor acknowledges, then accepts register address 0x75: its identity register.", "The controller issues a repeated start and the sensor address with a read direction.", "The sensor returns its identity byte.", "The controller sends NACK, not acknowledge, to request no further byte, then STOP; an earlier address NACK means no device accepted the request."],"Device address chooses the chip; register address chooses the value inside it."),
topic("Send a display command with clock and selection","SPI, serial peripheral interface, sends bits using a separate clock and a chip-select wire. In this connection the MAX7219 display chip receives a 16-bit command: eight bits select a register and eight specify its new value. Unlike I²C, this output path provides no acknowledgement.",'A display can accept a stream without device addresses or acknowledgements. Separate select wires choose receivers.',"Drive chip select CS low to begin a command for the display. Place each bit on MOSI, the controller-to-device data wire. Clock sixteen bits, starting with the most significant bit. Drive CS high to latch the complete register and data word. The display applies that command; software has no returned acknowledgement proving reception.",'PA5 SCK, PA7 MOSI, PB0 CS. Course SPI mode 0, DS=1111 for 16-bit frames, DR accessed as uint16_t; register is high byte and data is low byte. MAX7219 is write-only in this wiring; successful transmission is not confirmation that a chip received. MPU6050 is digital I²C here; ADC handles analog PA4.','Step all sixteen bits; identify when the display register changes, rather than when the first bit leaves.',source(9,'Main.c'),'static void max_cmd(uint8_t reg, uint8_t val) { spi_send16((uint16_t)((reg << 8) | val)); }',["Drive chip select CS low to begin a command for the display.", "Place each bit on MOSI, the controller-to-device data wire.", "Clock sixteen bits, starting with the most significant bit.", "Drive CS high to latch the complete register and data word.", "The display applies that command; software has no returned acknowledgement proving reception."],"Sixteen clocks deliver the command; the rising chip-select edge latches it."),
topic("Turn sensor readings into a moving display","A complete sensor application connects several jobs: read acceleration, update motion, draw pixels, and report measurements. The digital sensor has already converted motion into numbers; the ADC separately converts the knob’s voltage. Software still must assign units and keep shared bus access orderly.",'Raw sensor bytes need units, interpretation, scheduling and error handling before they mean motion.',"Wake the motion sensor and read its acceleration bytes every 20 ms. Combine each high and low byte into a signed reading and interpret its scale. Use acceleration to change velocity, then velocity to change position in fractional-pixel integer units. Apply wall collisions and send only changed display rows; use the knob reading for brightness. Copy a consistent sensor reading for 100 ms telemetry while locks prevent competing bus or output operations.",'Default ±2g MPU6050 scale is 16384 counts/g. Temperature formula raw/340+36.53 °C. bus_lock serializes shell and sensor I²C use; print_lock serializes lines. Digital sensors provide already-quantized bytes, unlike the ADC’s analog input.','Find which step should fail if the MPU remains asleep and why an ACK alone does not prove useful sensor readings.',source(9,'Main.c'),undefined,["Wake the motion sensor and read its acceleration bytes every 20 ms.", "Combine each high and low byte into a signed reading and interpret its scale.", "Use acceleration to change velocity, then velocity to change position in fractional-pixel integer units.", "Apply wall collisions and send only changed display rows; use the knob reading for brightness.", "Copy a consistent sensor reading for 100 ms telemetry while locks prevent competing bus or output operations."],"A sensor’s accepted address proves connection, not that it is awake or producing useful readings.")],prediction:{question:'Why does a controller NACK the final byte of an I²C read?',options:['The byte was corrupted','It signals that no more bytes are needed','The sensor address was wrong'],correct:1,explanations:['I²C ACK is not a checksum. Last-byte NACK is expected.','Correct: finish reading, then STOP.','Wrong-address NACK happens at address selection, before data.']},recap:['ADC measures voltage; I²C and SPI move already-digital bytes.','ACK proves acceptance at that stage, not a healthy sensor.','Pin, clock, control, status, data and bounded waiting recur in every peripheral.'],terms:[{term:'ADC',meaning:'Analog-to-digital converter: quantizes a voltage.'},{term:'I²C',meaning:'Inter-integrated circuit: addressed two-wire bus.'},{term:'SPI',meaning:'Serial peripheral interface: selected-device clocked shifting.'},{term:'ACK/NACK',meaning:'Acknowledge / not acknowledge on a ninth I²C clock.'},{term:"VDDA / VREFINT",meaning:"Analog supply used as the ADC reference here / internal reference voltage whose factory reading helps estimate that supply."},{term:"Open drain / pull-up",meaning:"The output drives low or releases the wire; a resistor brings the released wire high."},{term:"CS / SCK / MOSI",meaning:"Chip select chooses an SPI receiver; serial clock times its bits; controller output carries the bits to the device."},{term:"Big endian",meaning:"A multi-byte value sends or stores its most significant byte first."},{term:"Quantization",meaning:"Mapping a continuous input to one of a finite number of numeric codes."}],sources:[reference,cmsis,'https://www.analog.com/media/en/technical-documentation/data-sheets/MAX7219-MAX7221.pdf'],limitations:['ADC transfer model omits analog errors and settling.','Course ADC/bus Wokwi outcomes are predictions in its Lab; factory calibration may not be populated.','SPI experiment models course mode 0; other modes are not emulated.']},
{id:10,start:{"problem": "The processor lacks hardware floating-point arithmetic and otherwise copies each ADC sample itself.", "outcome": "Keep scaled-integer arithmetic meaningful and explain which copying work DMA removes.", "firstExperiment": ["In Number representations set Value A to 0.5; compare stored Q15 16384 with decoded 0.5.", "Open Arithmetic hazards and press Try addition overflow; observe the negative wrapped sum, then enable Saturating addition.", "Open DMA and CPU and toggle DMA transfer requested; step the frames to compare CPU copying with independent calculation."]},shortTitle:'Fractions and automatic data transfers',subtitle:'Fixed point and DMA: scaled numbers and hardware data transfers',prerequisites:[1,6,9],youKnow:['Numbers occupy finite bits.','ADC produces data and software can store it in SRAM.'],adds:['Scaled integers, rounding, saturation and arithmetic width','Honest performance comparisons','DMA, DMAMUX and circular ADC buffers'],why:'The Cortex-M0+ has no floating-point unit (FPU), hardware for decimal-style arithmetic. Software must still calculate fractions correctly and move samples without wasting processor time.',topics:[
topic("Represent fractions with scaled integers","Fixed point represents a fraction using an integer and a fixed scale agreed by the program. The stored integer is called the raw value. Q15 uses scale 32768: raw 16384 means 16384 divided by 32768, or 0.5. The bits do not store that scale, so code must keep its meaning consistent.",'Integer 0 cannot express half a unit, but multiplying all values by a scale preserves fractions.',"Choose the range and smallest fraction the calculation needs. Choose a format and scale: Q15 uses 32768, while Q16.16 uses 65536; 0.5 therefore stores as 16384 or 32768. Multiply the desired number by the scale and round to a representable raw integer. Store the integer within the format’s signed limits. Divide by the same scale to interpret it; fractions smaller than one stored step may disappear.",'Q1.15 uses signed 16-bit raw values: −1 through 32767/32768. Q16.16 uses signed 32 bits: −32768 through 32767.9999847. float has exponent-dependent spacing; int32 has unit steps. Course Q15 constant macro itself does not clamp invalid constants: use valid range.','Change a number by less than one Q15 step. Find when stored raw bits change.',source(10,'fix.h'),undefined,["Choose the range and smallest fraction the calculation needs.", "Choose a format and scale: Q15 uses 32768, while Q16.16 uses 65536; 0.5 therefore stores as 16384 or 32768.", "Multiply the desired number by the scale and round to a representable raw integer.", "Store the integer within the format’s signed limits.", "Divide by the same scale to interpret it; fractions smaller than one stored step may disappear."],"Q15 value = raw integer ÷ 32768; the program supplies the scale."),
topic("Calculate widely, then return to the chosen scale","A multiplication of two scaled integers produces a result with both scales multiplied together. A wider temporary integer preserves the product before rounding and rescaling. Saturation means clamping a result to the nearest representable limit; wrapping discards overflowing bits and can reverse its sign.",'Dropping high product bits or forgetting the second scale changes the physical meaning of the answer.',"Multiply two Q15 raw integers in a 32-bit temporary, producing scale 32768 squared. Add 16384 to implement the course’s rounding rule. Shift right fifteen bits to divide the scale back to 32768. Clamp the answer to the signed Q15 range before storing sixteen bits. For −1 times −1 the mathematical answer is +1, which must clamp because Q15 cannot represent +1 exactly.",'q15_mul: p=(int32_t)a*b; p+=1<<14; saturate(p>>15). Rounding ties go toward +∞ in this expression. Q16.16 multiply needs 64-bit intermediate and >>16. Division scales numerator before divide, with explicit divide-by-zero policy.','Set both addends to 0.8; compare wrap vs saturation. Then multiply −1 by −1.',source(10,'fix.h'),'int32_t p = (int32_t)a * b;\np += 1 << 14;\nreturn q15_sat32(p >> 15);',["Multiply two Q15 raw integers in a 32-bit temporary, producing scale 32768 squared.", "Add 16384 to implement the course’s rounding rule.", "Shift right fifteen bits to divide the scale back to 32768.", "Clamp the answer to the signed Q15 range before storing sixteen bits.", "For −1 times −1 the mathematical answer is +1, which must clamp because Q15 cannot represent +1 exactly."],"Keep the wide result first; round, rescale, and clamp before storing it."),
topic("Balance accuracy, memory, and calculation time","A lookup table stores precomputed answers; interpolation estimates a value between neighboring entries. A filter combines several weighted readings, so its accumulator, the temporary sum of products, may need more range than the final result. Speed comparisons are useful only when answers meet the required accuracy.",'A correct final format does not prevent intermediate overflow. Comparing speed without accuracy can reward a broken answer.',"Define the allowed numerical error and range before comparing implementations. For sine, fold a full turn into a 65-entry quarter-wave table, locate neighboring entries, and interpolate between them. For a finite impulse response (FIR) filter, multiply each reading by its coefficient and accumulate in a wide temporary. Check intermediate range as well as final rounding error. Compare integer, Q15, Q16.16, and float with changing inputs and consumed outputs; subtract measurement overhead and label timing-model cycles separately from device measurements.",'C031 M0+ has no FPU and no integer divide instruction; GCC calls software helpers. Benchmark subtracts loop/measurement overhead using SysTick. host/m0sim.py supplies a timing model with configurable Flash waits; these are model cycles, not silicon measurements. A compiler can fold a constant expression, so benchmarks require live inputs and sinks.','Explain why Q16.16 division may cost more than expected despite being called fixed point. Inspect bench.c and host kernel before accepting a leaderboard.',source(10,'bench.c'),undefined,["Define the allowed numerical error and range before comparing implementations.", "For sine, fold a full turn into a 65-entry quarter-wave table, locate neighboring entries, and interpolate between them.", "For a finite impulse response (FIR) filter, multiply each reading by its coefficient and accumulate in a wide temporary.", "Check intermediate range as well as final rounding error.", "Compare integer, Q15, Q16.16, and float with changing inputs and consumed outputs; subtract measurement overhead and label timing-model cycles separately from device measurements."],"A fast calculation is useful only if its result and intermediate values remain correct."),
topic("Move ADC readings into memory using DMA","Direct memory access (DMA) is hardware that copies data between configured addresses without making the CPU execute each copy. A bus master is a component allowed to initiate memory transfers. The DMA request multiplexer (DMAMUX) routes a peripheral’s transfer request to a DMA channel.",'The CPU otherwise spends its time copying samples instead of using them.',"Configure the ADC data register as source, the RAM buffer as destination, and the sample count and widths. Route ADC request 5 through DMAMUX channel 0 to DMA1 channel 1. Each completed ADC conversion requests a transfer; DMA reads the same ADC register and writes a halfword to the next incrementing RAM slot. After sampling PA0, PA1, and PA4, circular mode returns to the buffer’s beginning. The CPU can calculate meanwhile, but must coordinate reads with buffer updates and recover if ADC overrun makes sample order uncertain.",'CPAR source, CMAR destination, CNDTR count, CCR width/direction/increment/circular control. C031 has three DMA channels. Course fills scan_buf with 0xFFFF, impossible for 12-bit ADC, waits for all entries to change, and falls back to polling on timeout. OVR triggers recovery because channel order cannot be trusted.','Switch CPU-copy/DMA and step the same samples. Identify whether the CPU’s current operation is a copy or independent work.',source(10,'dma.c'),'DMA1_Channel1->CPAR = (uint32_t)&ADC1->DR;\nDMA1_Channel1->CMAR = (uint32_t)scan_buf;\nDMA1_Channel1->CNDTR = SCAN_CH;',["Configure the ADC data register as source, the RAM buffer as destination, and the sample count and widths.", "Route ADC request 5 through DMAMUX channel 0 to DMA1 channel 1.", "Each completed ADC conversion requests a transfer; DMA reads the same ADC register and writes a halfword to the next incrementing RAM slot.", "After sampling PA0, PA1, and PA4, circular mode returns to the buffer’s beginning.", "The CPU can calculate meanwhile, but must coordinate reads with buffer updates and recover if ADC overrun makes sample order uncertain."],"DMA copies each sample; software still configures transfers and coordinates buffer use."),
topic("Separate simulated behavior from measured speed","A numerical test checks answers. An architectural timing model estimates time from instruction behavior. A simulator implements selected hardware behavior, and a real board supplies physical measurements. These are different kinds of evidence; omitted hardware cannot be made real by reporting a simulated time.",'A simulator may omit DMA entirely or model timing differently. Reported microseconds are not automatically physical measurements.',"First test that the numerical algorithm returns correct answers. Inspect emitted instructions and memory use rather than assuming the source expression’s cost. Try the DMA transfer with an impossible ADC value, 0xFFFF, placed in every buffer slot. If values change, transfer activity occurred; if they remain unchanged until the bounded wait ends, fall back to CPU polling. Measure speed with a stated environment and overhead; changed buffer values alone do not establish throughput.",'Course Slide/Lab say Wokwi C031 DMA is not implemented; dma.c conservatively describes support as unconfirmed and probes. Here DMA is an educational hardware model. No browser throughput number is presented as measured hardware performance.','Explain what evidence a changing 0xFFFF sentinel provides and what it does not prove about speed.',source(10,'dma.c'),undefined,["First test that the numerical algorithm returns correct answers.", "Inspect emitted instructions and memory use rather than assuming the source expression’s cost.", "Try the DMA transfer with an impossible ADC value, 0xFFFF, placed in every buffer slot.", "If values change, transfer activity occurred; if they remain unchanged until the bounded wait ends, fall back to CPU polling.", "Measure speed with a stated environment and overhead; changed buffer values alone do not establish throughput."],"Changed data proves a transfer occurred; a clocked measurement is needed to prove its speed.")],prediction:{question:'Q15 stores −1 exactly. What must happen for (−1)×(−1)?',options:['Store +32768 in int16','Clamp to 32767, the greatest representable Q15 raw value','Return zero'],correct:1,explanations:['32768 cannot fit signed int16 and would wrap to −32768.','Correct: +1 is just outside Q15’s range, so saturate.','Multiplication is positive; zero would be an arithmetic error.']},recap:['Fixed-point bits acquire meaning from scale.','Widen, calculate, round, rescale, then saturate.','DMA removes per-sample CPU copying, not all CPU work or bus contention.'],terms:[{term:'Q15',meaning:'Signed 16-bit representation with fifteen fractional bits.'},{term:'Saturation',meaning:'Clamp an out-of-range answer to the nearest limit.'},{term:'DMA',meaning:'Direct memory access: a configured hardware bus master moves data.'},{term:'DMAMUX',meaning:'DMA request multiplexer: routes a peripheral’s transfer request to a selected DMA channel.'},{term:"Raw value",meaning:"The stored integer before dividing by its agreed scale."},{term:"FPU / FIR",meaning:"Floating-point unit, hardware arithmetic for floating values / finite impulse response, a filter combining a bounded set of weighted samples."},{term:"Accumulator / guard bits",meaning:"Temporary running sum / extra integer range reserved so intermediate sums do not overflow."},{term:"Interpolation",meaning:"Estimating a value between known neighboring table entries."},{term:"Sentinel",meaning:"Recognizable initial value used to tell whether memory has been overwritten."},{term:"Halfword / circular buffer",meaning:"A sixteen-bit value / a buffer whose write position returns to its beginning after reaching the end."}],sources:[reference,arm,cmsis],limitations:['No cycle or bandwidth result in this browser is a hardware measurement.','DMA is educational; the pinned course documents missing Wokwi DMA support.','Fixed arithmetic experiment ports bounded Q15 expressions; not a full C numerical emulator.']},
{id:11,start:{"problem": "A remote device must retain useful failure clues and restart when its required work stops.", "outcome": "Distinguish faults, silent corruption, and hangs, then choose feeding that proves job completion.", "firstExperiment": ["In Crash evidence select Stack overruns into valid SRAM and step through the saved evidence.", "Open Watchdog honesty with Independent IWDG, All job heartbeats, and One job stops completing selected.", "Press Advance ¼ timeout until the counter expires, then choose Every SysTick ISR and repeat to see feeding hide that missing job."]},shortTitle:'Recovering from faults and saving power',subtitle:'Faults, watchdogs, and power: detect failure and wait during idle time',prerequisites:[0,1,4,6,7],youKnow:['CPU executes instructions using addresses and stacks.','Reset initializes RAM sections; an interrupt saves a frame.'],adds:['Fault evidence and retained crash records','Independent and software watchdogs','Honest feeding and sleeping'],why:'Embedded software must diagnose failure and recover when nobody is there to restart it.',topics:[
topic("Collect evidence when the processor faults","A fault is a failure that prevents the processor from executing normally. HardFault is the Cortex-M0+ exception for such failures. A hang is different: useful work stops, possibly while instructions keep executing. The handler saves evidence such as the next instruction address before reset can erase it.",'Reset alone destroys many clues; failure may recur without anyone knowing its cause.',"An invalid instruction or unsupported memory access prevents normal execution. Hardware saves eight register values on the interrupted stack. The handler locates that frame and saves additional register evidence. Software inspects the saved program counter PC, instruction, and possible accessed address. Those clues support a diagnosis; a hang instead needs evidence of missing progress because no fault handler necessarily ran.",'Cortex-M0+ is ARMv6-M: it has HardFault but no ARMv7-M CFSR/BFAR diagnostic registers. EXC_RETURN bit 2 selects original MSP/PSP frame. Report registers are evidence, and classifier verdicts are reasoned hypotheses. A C divide by zero is undefined behavior, not a guaranteed hardware divide exception on this no-divide core.','Select a fault or hang. Before reset, identify what evidence is available and why a hang lacks a faulting PC.',source(11,'diag.c'),undefined,["An invalid instruction or unsupported memory access prevents normal execution.", "Hardware saves eight register values on the interrupted stack.", "The handler locates that frame and saves additional register evidence.", "Software inspects the saved program counter PC, instruction, and possible accessed address.", "Those clues support a diagnosis; a hang instead needs evidence of missing progress because no fault handler necessarily ran."],"A fault has a saved execution frame; a hang may only show that required work stopped."),
topic("Recognize memory corruption without a fault","Memory corruption means software changed data it did not intend to change. If a stack extends into neighboring writable SRAM, the processor can accept the address even though the program’s allocation was exceeded. A retained crash record is useful only if startup preserves it and its validation checks succeed.",'A system that did not HardFault can still have corrupted state. Valid address and valid program intent are different tests.',"A function uses more stack space than its allocation permits. The next write lands in another object that also occupies legal SRAM. The processor accepts the write, so no hardware fault is required. A damaged neighbor or changed stack marker reveals the error. After a software reset, inspect a validated .noinit record and reset flags; power loss may have destroyed the RAM evidence.",'The course memory classifier is coarse and does not prove every address within peripheral regions is implemented. Flight recorder .noinit is excluded from startup clearing, with magic/checksum validation; RCC flags independently identify reset causes. Retention is not promised after power loss.','Choose stack overwrite; predict whether a hardware exception is inevitable. Follow crash.c packet_sum exercise to reason from the saved load address.',source(11,'crash.c'),undefined,["A function uses more stack space than its allocation permits.", "The next write lands in another object that also occupies legal SRAM.", "The processor accepts the write, so no hardware fault is required.", "A damaged neighbor or changed stack marker reveals the error.", "After a software reset, inspect a validated .noinit record and reset flags; power loss may have destroyed the RAM evidence."],"A legal memory address can still belong to the wrong object."),
topic("Reset when progress stops","A watchdog is a countdown timer that resets the system unless software refreshes it in time. Refreshing is often called feeding. A software watchdog depends on timer-interrupt code running; an independent hardware watchdog has its own oscillator and can continue when those interrupts stop.",'An infinite loop can keep executing forever without ever producing output. Exceptions alone cannot catch it.',"Configure a timeout longer than the longest legitimate interval between successful checks. Start the watchdog counter. Successful work permits a refresh that reloads the counter. If work stops and no valid refresh occurs, the counter reaches its reset condition. An interrupt-driven software counter freezes when interrupts are masked; independent IWDG hardware keeps counting.",'IWDG KR keys: 0xCCCC start, 0x5555 unlock configuration, 0xAAAA refresh. Timeout=(RLR+1)×(4<<PR)/fLSI. LSI nominal 32 kHz is not precise timing. Once enabled, hardware dogs cannot simply be turned off. Software dog fails if interrupts stop.','Mask interrupts during a hang. Compare Soft vs IWDG: which still has a ticking clock?',source(11,'wdog.c'),'IWDG->KR = KEY_START;\nIWDG->KR = KEY_UNLOCK;\nIWDG->KR = KEY_FEED;',["Configure a timeout longer than the longest legitimate interval between successful checks.", "Start the watchdog counter.", "Successful work permits a refresh that reloads the counter.", "If work stops and no valid refresh occurs, the counter reaches its reset condition.", "An interrupt-driven software counter freezes when interrupts are masked; independent IWDG hardware keeps counting."],"Recovery requires a timer that still runs during the failure you want to detect."),
topic("Refresh only after required jobs finish","A heartbeat is a recorded sign that one required job completed. Each job has a separate bit in a shared record. Feeding only after all expected bits appear proves more than a running interrupt or a spinning main loop: it proves that every required job finished in this check round.",'An ISR can keep feeding while main is stuck; a top-of-loop feed can hide one missing job.',"Start a check round with no jobs recorded. Each required job sets its own heartbeat bit after finishing. Compare the collected bits with the expected job mask. Refresh the watchdog only when every expected bit is present, then clear the record for a new round. If one job stops, its bit remains missing and the watchdog can expire even when other code still runs.",'hb_ready tests (seen & expected)==expected. A feed from SysTick every millisecond falsely proves only that SysTick still runs. Course tests compare ISR, loop and heartbeat policies with loop hang, masked-interrupt hang and starved job.','Select a stalled job and compare feeding policies. Explain why ISR feed can prevent necessary recovery.',source(11,'wdog.h'),'if ((h->seen & h->expected) != h->expected) { return 0; }\nh->seen = 0;',["Start a check round with no jobs recorded.", "Each required job sets its own heartbeat bit after finishing.", "Compare the collected bits with the expected job mask.", "Refresh the watchdog only when every expected bit is present, then clear the record for a new round.", "If one job stops, its bit remains missing and the watchdog can expire even when other code still runs."],"Feed after completed work from every required job, not merely after another instruction ran."),
topic("Wait during idle time","Idle time is the interval when no useful work is ready. WFI means Wait For Interrupt, an instruction that suspends core instruction execution until a wake condition. In ordinary Sleep, enabled peripherals may keep running, allowing an interrupt to wake the CPU for its next job.",'Spinning does the same useful work as sleeping—none—but consumes more switching energy.',"Complete the work due at the current time. If nothing else is ready, execute WFI instead of repeatedly polling. The core suspends instruction execution while configured peripherals may continue. A wake condition resumes execution and software checks the next work. The useful-work percentage stays the same; reduced idle switching can lower real-device energy use, which needs a physical measurement to quantify.",'Course measures busy vs idle intervals using SysTick; computed CPU load is not a current measurement. Wokwi does not establish physical power saving. Deeper Stop/Standby require chip-specific clock, wake and retention planning, not just inserting WFI.','Compare sleep and spin with identical work schedule. Predict unchanged useful CPU-load accounting but different real-device power.',source(11,'Main.c'),undefined,["Complete the work due at the current time.", "If nothing else is ready, execute WFI instead of repeatedly polling.", "The core suspends instruction execution while configured peripherals may continue.", "A wake condition resumes execution and software checks the next work.", "The useful-work percentage stays the same; reduced idle switching can lower real-device energy use, which needs a physical measurement to quantify."],"The same useful workload can use less idle energy when the CPU waits instead of spins.")],prediction:{question:'Why is feeding from SysTick unsafe when main is stuck?',options:['SysTick necessarily stops','It can keep feeding although useful work stopped','A watchdog can only be fed from assembly'],correct:1,explanations:['A main-loop hang may leave interrupts working normally.','Correct. Heartbeats must prove completion of required jobs.','C stores to a watchdog key register can feed it; the policy is the issue.']},recap:['A crash, hang and silent corruption require different evidence.','A watchdog is only as useful as its feeding policy.','Waiting instead of polling reduces unnecessary processor activity on real hardware. The browser’s useful-work percentage cannot measure that energy saving.'],terms:[{term:'HardFault',meaning:'ARMv6-M exception for faults that prevent normal execution.'},{term:'Watchdog',meaning:'Countdown timer that forces recovery unless correctly refreshed.'},{term:'.noinit',meaning:'RAM section deliberately excluded from startup initialization.'},{term:'WFI',meaning:'Wait For Interrupt instruction used to suspend idle execution.'},{term:"PC / EXC_RETURN",meaning:"Program counter identifies the instruction position; exception-return encoding tells the processor which mode and stack to resume."},{term:"Heartbeat",meaning:"Recorded evidence that a required job finished in the current check round."},{term:"IWDG / WWDG / LSI",meaning:"Independent watchdog / window watchdog / low-speed internal oscillator used by the independent watchdog."},{term:"Hang / corruption",meaning:"Loss of required progress / unintended alteration of data, which may occur without a hardware exception."},{term:"CFSR / BFAR",meaning:"Configurable fault status register / bus fault address register; these ARMv7-M fault-detail registers are absent on this ARMv6-M core."}],sources:[reference,arm,cmsis,'https://docs.wokwi.com/parts/board-st-nucleo-c031c6'],limitations:['Fault paths are architectural teaching sequences, not browser execution of unsafe C.','Pinned course: IWDG not modeled by Wokwi; WWDG listed implemented but not tested. Soft dog is course workaround.','Crash classification is not guaranteed diagnosis; power saving is not measured here.']},
{id:12,start:{"problem": "Game logic changes RAM quickly, but the display sees changes only after a limited-speed bus transfer.", "outcome": "Trace input through game state, framebuffer, and display memory, and explain the page-transfer cost.", "firstExperiment": ["In Frame pipeline move Target Y from 20 to 30.", "Press Next event through Framebuffer changes and Display RAM changes; compare when each memory’s point moves.", "Open Pixel & bus budget, set Y to 40, press Draw into RAM, then Flush to OLED; watch the two points become equal and dirty pages clear."]},shortTitle:'Building a small game',subtitle:'Game: connect input, game logic, and a 128×64 display',prerequisites:[5,6,8,9,10],youKnow:['I²C sends bytes with ACK clocks.','A fixed-point value preserves fractional position. Inputs have held levels and press edges.'],adds:['Pure game core and thin hardware platform','Framebuffer bits and dirty-page transport','Fixed update timing, state machines and replay'],why:'An arcade turns inputs, logic, memory and bus timing into one visible embedded system.',topics:[
topic("Draw in RAM, then send pixels to the screen","A framebuffer is RAM containing the intended pixel values. The organic light-emitting diode (OLED) display has its own controller memory. Drawing changes the framebuffer; a flush is the transfer that copies updated bytes to the display. The two memories can temporarily contain different pictures.",'A CPU must send a new image over a limited bus; changing a variable alone cannot change external pixels.',"Sample input and update game position on a 20 ms logic tick. Erase the previous pixel and set the new pixel in the RAM framebuffer. Mark each changed eight-row display page for transfer. Send commands and pixel bytes over I²C to the SSD1306 controller. The controller updates its own display memory and refreshes the visible screen.",'128×64 bits =1024 pixel bytes. Pixel (x,y) is byte fb[y>>3][1+x], bit y&7; extra [0] byte is 0x40 control. 8 pages ×128 columns. I²C address 0x3C; 0x00 introduces commands, 0x40 introduces pixels.','Select a pixel. Compute its page and bit, then flush; observe RAM and display as separate states.', '_lib/oled.c','uint8_t *b = &fb[y >> 3][1 + x];\nuint8_t m = (uint8_t)(1u << (y & 7));',["Sample input and update game position on a 20 ms logic tick.", "Erase the previous pixel and set the new pixel in the RAM framebuffer.", "Mark each changed eight-row display page for transfer.", "Send commands and pixel bytes over I²C to the SSD1306 controller.", "The controller updates its own display memory and refreshes the visible screen."],"Drawing changes CPU RAM; flushing changes display RAM."),
topic("Send changed pages instead of a full screen","A display page is an eight-pixel-high strip covering 128 columns. Dirty means its framebuffer bytes changed and await transfer. Sending only dirty pages reduces bus time, but this driver still sends a complete page when only one of its pixels changed.",'Full frames can take longer than the 20 ms game update period. The bus, not drawing, may be the bottleneck.',"Change a framebuffer byte and set its page’s dirty bit. At flush time, skip pages whose bits are clear. For each dirty page, send its address window and all 128 pixel bytes. Clear that page’s dirty bit only after the transfer succeeds, allowing retry on failure. Compare the transfer time with the 20 ms update interval; sending all eight pages exceeds it at the course’s 400 kHz clock.",'Page window transfer: address+0x00+6 commands=8 bytes. Pixel transfer: address+0x40+128 pixels=130. Total 138 bytes×9 clocks. Ideal 400 kHz lower bound=3.105 ms/page, 24.84 ms/8 pages, excluding START/STOP/software overhead. Course timing model includes overhead (~24.92 ms).','Toggle full vs dirty transport and bus speed. Explain why final pixels can match but frame deadlines differ.','_lib/oled.c','if (*b != old) { dirty |= (uint8_t)(1u << (y >> 3)); }',["Change a framebuffer byte and set its page’s dirty bit.", "At flush time, skip pages whose bits are clear.", "For each dirty page, send its address window and all 128 pixel bytes.", "Clear that page’s dirty bit only after the transfer succeeds, allowing retry on failure.", "Compare the transfer time with the 20 ms update interval; sending all eight pages exceeds it at the course’s 400 kHz clock."],"One changed pixel marks one page; one dirty page sends 128 pixel bytes plus commands."),
topic("Advance game time in equal steps","A fixed timestep means each game update advances the same modeled time, here 20 ms. Rendering means drawing the current state; flushing means sending it to the screen. These can take variable time without changing the meaning of each logic step, provided the loop handles delays within a bounded catch-up policy.",'Variable steps can make a ball tunnel through obstacles and make playback depend on machine speed.',"Read debounced input, which removes spurious rapid changes from mechanical buttons. Run each due 20 ms logic update with the recorded held and newly pressed inputs. If behind, perform bounded catch-up updates; the course discards excessive backlog rather than catching up forever. Draw the latest state into RAM and flush its changed pages. A slow flush can reduce visible refresh rate while logic still uses equal time steps.",'game_t provides start, step, draw, score. Pure game code never reads registers or clocks. Main.c owns timing and hardware. pad_read debounces and supplies down/pressed/released; stick zones avoid noisy center movement. Sound is a nonblocking timer-backed sequence through sfx().','Increase full transport cost beyond 20 ms. Distinguish update rate from achieved display refresh rate.',source(12,'engine.h'),'#define TICK_MS   20',["Read debounced input, which removes spurious rapid changes from mechanical buttons.", "Run each due 20 ms logic update with the recorded held and newly pressed inputs.", "If behind, perform bounded catch-up updates; the course discards excessive backlog rather than catching up forever.", "Draw the latest state into RAM and flush its changed pages.", "A slow flush can reduce visible refresh rate while logic still uses equal time steps."],"Logic time advances by 20 ms per update; display time depends on drawing and transport."),
topic("Build games from bounded data structures","Each game stores a compact state: Snake tracks occupied cells, Breakout tracks bricks and rectangles, and Flap tracks position, velocity, and pipes. A pure game core reads inputs and changes this state without reading hardware registers or clocks, so the same code can run in host tests.",'Simple finite state and bounded memory make behavior predictable on a small MCU.',"Start the game with a known seed, the initial number used by its repeatable random generator. Apply the next recorded input on each fixed update. Snake adds a head, removes a tail, and checks occupied cells; Breakout tests rectangles and clears brick bits; Flap adds gravity to velocity and velocity to position, with a press setting upward velocity. Draw the resulting state through platform functions that host tests can replace. Replay the same seed and input sequence to obtain the same state and framebuffer hashes.",'xorshift32 uses shifts 13,17,5 for deterministic replay. Collision uses four interval comparisons; touching edges need not overlap. const sprite/font arrays stay in Flash on ARM without AVR PROGMEM. Course host tests play actual games with scripted inputs, framebuffer hashes and bus-counting stub.','Change seed and replay; explain why same seed plus same input sequence yields same result.',source(12,'engine.c'),undefined,["Start the game with a known seed, the initial number used by its repeatable random generator.", "Apply the next recorded input on each fixed update.", "Snake adds a head, removes a tail, and checks occupied cells; Breakout tests rectangles and clears brick bits; Flap adds gravity to velocity and velocity to position, with a press setting upward velocity.", "Draw the resulting state through platform functions that host tests can replace.", "Replay the same seed and input sequence to obtain the same state and framebuffer hashes."],"Same starting seed plus same ordered inputs gives the same pure-game result."),
topic("Choose valid inputs for each game state","A state machine records the current mode and permits specific transitions. Menu, play, pause, and game over therefore react differently to the same button. A press edge means the change from released to pressed, rather than every update while a button remains held. Finished scores are framed data for the host to validate.",'A frantic losing press should not immediately dismiss its result; unvalidated serial scores should not silently count.',"In the menu, a new start-button press begins a game. During play, fixed updates change the game; pause changes which updates are allowed. Game completion enters game over and briefly ignores buttons to keep the result visible; high-score text blinks by XOR and timer-driven sound continues independently. After the lockout, a permitted input can return to the menu. Send the score in a checksummed frame; the host validates it before recording the best score.",'Course host/leaderboard.py validates pasted score lines, keeps per-player/game best scores and reports invalid records. Example $SCORE,SNAKE,172*2E. Host results are observed algorithm/buffer behavior, not physical display refresh or speaker measurements.','Build a score frame with UART lesson tools and check it. Follow engine’s platform hook boundary to see what host test replaces.',source(12,'arcade.c'),undefined,["In the menu, a new start-button press begins a game.", "During play, fixed updates change the game; pause changes which updates are allowed.", "Game completion enters game over and briefly ignores buttons to keep the result visible; high-score text blinks by XOR and timer-driven sound continues independently.", "After the lockout, a permitted input can return to the menu.", "Send the score in a checksummed frame; the host validates it before recording the best score."],"Current mode determines what a button press can do; validate scores before storing them.")],prediction:{question:'Only one pixel changes. How much pixel storage must the course dirty-page driver resend?',options:['Exactly one bit','One full 128-byte page plus protocol overhead','Always all 1024 bytes'],correct:1,explanations:['This driver tracks changed pages, not individual pixel runs.','Correct. Finer diff strategies are possible, but not this driver’s contract.','Only dirty pages are sent unless explicitly forcing full repaint.']},recap:['RAM framebuffer and display RAM are separate places.','Identical final image does not imply identical bus traffic.','Game logic separated from hardware, with equal update steps, can replay the same ordered inputs and produce the same result.'],terms:[{term:'Framebuffer',meaning:'RAM holding the pixel values to be displayed.'},{term:'Dirty page',meaning:'Changed eight-pixel-high display strip awaiting transport.'},{term:'Fixed timestep',meaning:'Every logic update advances the same amount of modeled time.'},{term:'AABB',meaning:'Axis-aligned bounding box: rectangle used for simple overlap tests.'},{term:"Flush",meaning:"Copy changed framebuffer pages from CPU RAM to display-controller RAM."},{term:"OLED",meaning:"Organic light-emitting diode display; its controller stores and refreshes received pixels."},{term:"Press edge / debounce",meaning:"A transition from released to pressed / filtering rapid electrical changes caused by mechanical button contacts."},{term:"Pure game core",meaning:"Logic that changes game data using inputs, without reading hardware registers or clocks."},{term:"Seed / replay",meaning:"Starting value for repeatable pseudo-random choices / running the same ordered inputs again."},{term:"State machine",meaning:"A stored mode plus rules for which events move the system to another mode."}],sources:[reference,'https://cdn-shop.adafruit.com/datasheets/SSD1306.pdf'],limitations:['Interactive moving pixel is a teaching example, not a port of the three complete course games. Actual game sources and host tests are linked.','Bus budget is calculated ideal timing, not measured hardware FPS.','Educational pipeline separates updates for inspection; real loop executes continuously.']}
];
export default systems;

export const lessons = systems;
